@h1 Embed the Wit parser h1@

Run the Wit pipeline inside your own program — parse a document, feed it your own
data, get HTML back — with no CLI, no subprocess, and no config file. This is for
developers.

@h2 Step 1 — Install h2@

Add the three packages you need:

@@@pre
npm install @witlang/parser @witlang/runtime @witlang/render-html
pre@@@

@h2 Step 2 — Run the whole pipeline h2@

The pipeline is @code parse code@, then @code loadExternalData code@ (only if the
document loads external data), then @code resolve code@, @code expand code@, and
finally a renderer. Verified end to end:

@@@pre
import { parse } from '@witlang/parser';
import { loadExternalData, resolve, expand } from '@witlang/runtime';
import { renderHtml } from '@witlang/render-html';

const src = `@load meta load@
@load sales load@

@h1 Release @meta.version h1@
Cut @meta.date.

@table |rows @sales|
`;

// Feed data through the same seam the CLI uses. A plain dictionary pairs each
// alias with an already-resolved value; keys must match the @load aliases.
const doc = loadExternalData(parse(src, 'report.wit'), {
  meta:  { version: '0.4.0', date: '2026-07-05' },
  sales: [{ site: 'Dunmore', lit: 'yes' }, { site: 'Harbour', lit: 'no' }],
});

const html = renderHtml(expand(resolve(doc)), { mode: 'document', title: 'Release' });
pre@@@

@strong Result. strong@ @code html code@ is a full page whose body contains
@@@code <h1>Release 0.4.0</h1><p>Cut 2026-07-05.</p> code@@@ and a table with the
two sales rows — verified. Pass @code mode: 'fragment' code@ for embedding, or
supply @code css code@ to override the inlined theme.

@h2 The data seam h2@

A data source is either a plain dictionary or a loader function. The dictionary
form is simplest; a function of the shape
@@@code (req) => value code@@@ — where @code req code@ carries the alias, its
arguments, and a source location — fetches lazily per alias.

@aside(class note) 💡 @code loadExternalData code@ @strong only rewrites
@@@code @load code@@@ uses. strong@ The document must contain the
@@@code @load code@@@ lines, and the dictionary keys must match those aliases.
Omit the @@@code @load meta load@ code@@@ line and
@@@code @meta.version code@@@ throws an unresolved-reference error. aside@

@h2 Multi-file documents h2@

If the source uses @code reference code@, pass resolve options with a
@code rootPath code@ and a @strong synchronous strong@ @code fileReader code@ that
takes an absolute path and returns the file's text. A single-file document needs
no options.

@aside(class note) 💡 Older reference prose shows an async @code readFile code@
option — the current signature is a @strong sync strong@ @code fileReader code@.
Use the current one. aside@

@h2 Pitfalls h2@

@ul
@li @strong No @code load code@ line, no data. strong@ Without the matching @@@code @load code@@@ line the dictionary key is never consulted, and the reference stays unresolved. li@
@li @strong References need a root. strong@ Calling @code resolve code@ on a document that uses @code reference code@ without a @code rootPath code@ throws — the resolver refuses to silently drop references. li@
ul@

